{"benchmark": "ifeval", "task_id": "1092", "sample_idx": 10, "model_name": "qwen/qwen3-next-80b-a3b-instruct", "content": "My Japan Trip in 300 Words\n\nI arrived in Kyoto just as cherry blossoms began to fall—a quiet storm of pink petals drifting over temple roofs. No guidebook could prepare me for the hush of Kinkaku-ji, the golden pavilion mirrored perfectly in still water, or the scent of incense curling through ancient cedar trees.\n\nI wandered Nishiki Market at dawn, nibbling on matcha mochi and salty pickled plums, while fishmongers called out in rhythmic cadence. A grandmother handed me a warm taiyaki without a word—just a smile and a nod. That moment stayed with me longer than any souvenir.\n\nIn Tokyo, the city pulsed like a living circuit board. Shibuya Crossing was a tidal wave of humanity, yet no one rushed. Everyone moved with purpose, yet with grace. I got lost in Akihabara’s neon glow, then found peace hours later in a tiny tea house in Yanaka, sipping hojicha as an old man played shamisen in the next room.\n\nThe bullet train to Hiroshima was silent, save for the hum of wheels and the occasional rustle of a newspaper. At the Peace Memorial, I placed a paper crane beside thousands of others—each one a whisper of hope. That night, I ate okonomiyaki under string lights in a back-alley stall, the chef asking if I liked it “with love or with fire.” I chose both.\n\nJapan doesn’t shout. It whispers—in the clink of chopsticks, the bow of a shopkeeper, the way a ryokan attendant leaves a single plum on your pillow before bed. I came for the temples and the tech. I left with stillness.\n\nI didn’t bring back much. Just a tea set, a postcard of Mount Fuji, and the quiet understanding that beauty lives in pauses.\n\nI’ll be back. Not to see more. But to feel more.", "logprobs": [{"token": "My", "logprob": -0.23346616327762604}, {"token": " Japan", "logprob": -0.10737951844930649}, {"token": " Trip", "logprob": -0.06732307374477386}, {"token": " in", "logprob"